WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.450
Different claims need different evidence.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.930
To show something can happen, one example is enough.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.270
To show something always happens or can never happen,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.470
your reasons must cover every case.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.630
A few examples only settle the cases that you checked.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.310
Patterns can hold for a long time and then break.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.620
Knowing what a claim actually requires stops you from

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.460
trusting a pattern too far, and tells you when your

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.010
solution is really complete.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.420
Here is a claim: the sum of two odd numbers

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.590
is always even.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.270
Three and five make eight.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:34.270
Seven and nine make sixteen.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.210
Eleven and one make twelve.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:37.590
Is that a proof?

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.090
Pause and decide.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.770
Three examples are not a proof, because there are

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.420
endlessly many odd numbers.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:49.060
Here is a reason instead.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.790
Any odd number is a collection of pairs with

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:52.740
one left over.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.080
Put two odd numbers together.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.880
Their two leftover counters make one more pair.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.140
Now everything is in pairs, so the total is even.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.480
This works for every pair of odd numbers at once.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.610
To show a claim is false, one example that breaks

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:09.460
it is enough.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.310
Someone claims that every odd number bigger than

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:13.280
one is prime.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.940
Three, five and seven fit.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.180
But nine is three times three.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.920
One counterexample and the claim is finished.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:22.820
One more distinction.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:25.740
A condition can be needed without being enough.

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:30.220
Every multiple of six is even, so being even is needed.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.960
But fourteen is even and is not a multiple of six,

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.100
so being even is not enough.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.120
One example shows something can happen.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.300
A reason covering every case shows it always does.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.160
One counterexample shows a claim is false.

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:48.770
Notebook challenge: is it true that the sum of three odd

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:50.180
numbers is always odd?

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.400
Give a reason, not just examples.
